<style>
  /*
    10mm margin fits safely inside both A4 (210x297mm) and US Letter
    (215.9x279.4mm) without needing to detect which one is active —
    print-time detection of the OS/printer's selected page size is
    inconsistently supported across browsers, so this design
    deliberately avoids depending on it.
  */
  @page {
    margin: 10mm;
  }
  body {
    background: #2b2b2b;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    align-items: center;
    padding: 2rem;
  }
  .controls-row {
    width: 560px;
    margin-bottom: 1.5rem;
    display: flex;
    justify-content: space-between;
    align-items: center;
  }
  .controls-row button, .controls-row a {
    padding: 0.5rem 1.5rem;
    font-size: 1rem;
    cursor: pointer;
    text-decoration: none;
    color: inherit;
    background: #e8ddc4;
    border: 2px solid #333;
    border-radius: 4px;
    font-family: Georgia, "Times New Roman", serif;
  }

  /*
    NOTE:
    On-screen only: shrinks the card to fit within 80% of the viewport
    height on short screens, instead of forcing a scrollbar. Same
    transform: scale() technique as the print rule below (a max-height
    alone would clip the card's fixed-height sections rather than
    shrinking them together), but the ratio is computed in JavaScript
    (see move_card_print_script.html.jinja2) rather than pure CSS
    calc()/min() — dividing two different CSS units (vh, px) into a
    plain number for scale() relies on CSS math-function support that
    isn't reliably available in every browser yet, confirmed not to
    take effect in at least one real test. --screen-scale is a custom
    property, not the transform property itself, specifically so the
    @media print rule below (a plain, self-contained transform
    declaration) keeps winning during printing through normal CSS
    cascade — an inline style directly on `transform` would otherwise
    always win over any stylesheet rule, print media query included.
  */
  .card {
    transform: scale(var(--screen-scale, 1));
    transform-origin: top center;
  }

  /*
    NOTE:
    A physically accurate print needs the card at its real ~63x88mm
    size (Magic-style), not the 560px width sized for comfortable
    screen viewing. Rather than rewriting every nested px value in
    move_card_style.html.jinja2 in mm (dozens of fixed heights/paddings
    across header/art/body-section/etc.), a single uniform transform:
    scale() on .card preserves every internal proportion exactly as
    designed on screen. The factor is derived from width only (560px ->
    63mm, using the standard 96px = 1in = 25.4mm conversion: 63 / (560 *
    25.4 / 96) ~= 0.4252) since width is the one dimension with no
    min-height ambiguity in the source CSS. This card's actual pixel
    ratio (~560:815) isn't a perfect match for 63:88 (the .art section
    uses min-height, not a fixed height), so the printed height comes
    out around 91-92mm rather than exactly 88mm — undistorted, just
    not pinned to both dimensions at once. Deliberately left as-is
    rather than adjusting the screen layout's fixed heights to true up
    the ratio, since that's already-approved visual design from an
    earlier session, not something to change for print's sake alone.
  */
  @media print {
    .controls-row {
      display: none;
    }
    body {
      background: none;
      padding: 0;
      align-items: flex-start;
    }
    .card {
      transform: scale(0.4252);
      transform-origin: top left;
    }
  }
</style>
